---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <div class="py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 md:px-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-8">About</h1>
      <div class="max-w-none">
        <p class="text-xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-8">
          ProductBuildersHQ exists to help people build products in the age of AI — products that
          ship, get adopted, and earn.
        </p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Our Mission</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-4">
          We believe the boundaries between Product Managers and Engineers are dissolving. AI is compressing implementation barriers, shifting value toward specification, validation, and operational ownership.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-4">
          The focus is <strong class="text-[var(--color-text)]">building, not training</strong>.
          The frameworks, the maturity models, the skills — these are means, not the end. The end
          is a shipped product with real results: adoption, revenue, growth. Learning matters only
          because it gets you there faster; when building is cheap,
          <a href="/frameworks/five-ps" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">only results are scarce</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
          So the frameworks help individuals and teams navigate this transition — whether you are a
          PM learning to build, an Engineer developing product judgment, or an organization evolving
          toward higher autonomy levels — but they exist to be used, not admired. The destination is
          the build.
        </p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">What We Publish</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-4 text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
          <li><strong class="text-[var(--color-text)]">Frameworks:</strong> Maturity models with levels, tools, metrics, and practices for growth.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[var(--color-text)]">Case Studies:</strong> Real-world implementations from companies like AWS, Spotify, and StrongDM.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-[var(--color-text)]">Products:</strong> Applied tools such as <a href="/products/visionspec" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">VisionSpec</a> that help teams turn strategy, specifications, and delivery workflows into operating systems.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Who&rsquo;s Behind ProductBuildersHQ</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-4">
          ProductBuildersHQ is created by <strong class="text-[var(--color-text)]">John Wang</strong>
          (<a href="https://github.com/grokify" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">grokify</a>) —
          a builder of developer tools and open-source infrastructure with
          <a href="https://github.com/grokify" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">600+ repositories on GitHub</a> and a
          top-0.01% <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/1908967/grokify" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">Stack Overflow</a> reputation —
          earning 2,000+ reputation in his first 30 days of posting, a method he presented at the
          CoderCruise conference as
          <a href="https://grokify.github.io/stackoverflow-the-hard-way/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline"><em>Stack Overflow the Hard Way</em></a> (2018).
          He is also the ProductBuilder behind
          <a href="https://plexusone.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">PlexusOne.dev</a>
          (<a href="https://github.com/plexusone" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">GitHub</a>) —
          100+ repositories of AI-agent and developer-experience infrastructure — and
          <a href="https://aistandards.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">AIStandards.io</a>
          (<a href="https://github.com/aistandardsio" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">GitHub</a>),
          focused on AI/ML interoperability standards.
          Connect on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/johncwang/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">LinkedIn</a> or
          <a href="https://x.com/grokify" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">X</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-4">
          For John, the ProductBuilder path has been a lifelong one. It began formally at the
          University of Pennsylvania&rsquo;s Management &amp; Technology Program — dual degrees
          across Engineering and Business, in Computer Science and Finance — and
          informally well before that. A solo founder, he has since crossed the
          product&ndash;engineering boundary that the
          <a href="/frameworks/product-builder-maturity-model" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">Product Builder Maturity Model</a> maps —
          in both directions: starting in engineering as a Member of Technical Staff, then
          rounds through software development, interoperability testing (partner quality
          engineering), sales and solutions engineering, product management, and product
          marketing — most recently platform product management, including product management
          for developer programs, APIs, and SDKs. A profile built over a lifetime — fluent in
          what to build, how it gets built, and how it reaches developers — that AI now makes
          decisive: the ProductBuilder. What that path compounds into is taste and discernment —
          earned opinion and proven expertise — the two inputs AI cannot supply. The code and
          the commands, it can.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-4">
          That perspective has been on stage as well: John has spoken at venues including AWS
          Builder Loft, Salesforce Dreamforce, Oracle OpenWorld, DevRelCon, and CoderCruise. He
          is available for talks on AI-native product development, the ProductBuilder role, and
          spec-driven development — a version of the current talk,
          <a href="/presentation" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">What is a ProductBuilder?</a>,
          is on this site, and his current thinking shows up first in
          <a href="/perspectives" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">Perspectives</a>.
          Invitations via
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/johncwang/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">LinkedIn</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
          The open-source ecosystem behind this site is part of that body of work: the feed
          aggregation, discussion analysis, navigation components, and specification tooling that
          power ProductBuildersHQ are built on the same repositories. And the tools published here —
          <a href="/products/visionstudio" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">VisionStudio</a>
          and the <a href="/sdd/definition/pbhq-lite" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">PBHQ Lite</a>
          workflow — are the same tools used to build
          <a href="https://plexusone.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">PlexusOne.dev</a>,
          <a href="https://aistandards.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">AIStandards.io</a>,
          this site, and VisionStudio itself.
          The frameworks here aren&rsquo;t admired; they&rsquo;re in production — written by someone
          who ships the machinery they describe.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-4">
          More than that, the perspective itself is downstream of the building. Most of what
          ProductBuildersHQ publishes &mdash; the frameworks, the maturity models, and the
          <a href="/perspectives" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">Perspectives</a> &mdash;
          developed out of the high-velocity AI development inside
          <a href="https://plexusone.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">PlexusOne</a>,
          particularly since output began compounding around December 2025. The credibility runs in
          that direction: the ideas are field notes from a working practice, not a practice
          reverse-engineered from ideas. Without PlexusOne, most of this content would never have
          existed.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Original ProductBuildersHQ IP</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-4">
          AI-native product development is a new field, and much of the foundational thinking is
          being written now. The following models, frameworks, specifications, and tools are
          original ProductBuildersHQ work — marked <span class="text-xs font-medium px-2 py-0.5 bg-[var(--color-accent)] text-[var(--color-primary-dark)] rounded-full whitespace-nowrap">PBHQ Original</span> across the site:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-6">
          <li><a href="/philosophy" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">The 4P Product-Building Philosophy</a> — Program, Practice, Product, Progress: the continuous-improvement loop we build by</li>
          <li><a href="/frameworks/product-loop-builder-loop" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">The Product Loop &amp; Builder Loop</a> — the two-loop operating model for AI-native product development</li>
          <li><a href="/frameworks/product-builder-maturity-model" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">Product Builder Maturity Model (PBMM)</a> — the person: Observer to Product Builder</li>
          <li><a href="/frameworks/software-delivery-autonomy" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">Software Delivery Autonomy Levels &amp; ASDM</a> — the process: Agile to Autonomous Operations</li>
          <li><a href="/frameworks/five-ps" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">The Five Ps</a> — the product: Preparation to Profit</li>
          <li><a href="/frameworks/app-reality-ladder" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">The App Reality Ladder</a> — the artifact: Concept to Production, graded by what it proves</li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/ProductBuildersHQ/lever" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">LEVER / REVEL</a> — the capability framework, read forward as the climb and backward as the goal cascade</li>
          <li><a href="/products/scale" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">SCALE</a> — Standards, Consumption, Automation, Leverage, Effectiveness</li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/ProductBuildersHQ/pdlc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">PDLC</a> — the product development lifecycle specification and Product Baseline handoff</li>
          <li><a href="/frameworks/loop-engineering" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">Loop Engineering</a> — designing self-running AI coding agents</li>
          <li><a href="/frameworks/ai-native-pm-metrics" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">AI-Native Product Manager Metrics</a> — outcome measures for the Product Loop</li>
          <li><a href="/frameworks/dora-space-ai-age" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">AI-DORA &amp; AI-SPACE</a> — original AI-era extensions of the DORA metrics and the SPACE framework</li>
          <li><a href="/products/visionstudio" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium">VisionSpec, VisionStudio &amp; PRISM</a> — the engine, studio, and control plane that operate the system, including the PBHQ Lite workflow</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Alongside our own work we publish <span class="text-xs font-medium px-2 py-0.5 bg-[var(--color-bg-secondary)] text-[var(--color-text-muted)] rounded-full whitespace-nowrap">Industry Analysis</span> of
          others&rsquo; frameworks (DORA, SPACE, the SDD tool landscape) and machine-readable
          <em>encodings</em> of established methodologies (Amazon Working Backwards, Shape Up,
          Lean Startup, and others) in the <a href="/sdd/" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">SDD Catalog</a> —
          always attributed, and never implying affiliation or endorsement by their originators.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Living Documents</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Our frameworks are living documents, updated quarterly as tools evolve and new patterns emerge. Each document includes version numbers and update dates for transparency.
        </p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Contact</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
          Have feedback on our frameworks? Found a case study we should cover? Reach out on
          <a href="https://github.com/ProductBuildersHQ" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline">GitHub</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
